1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This edition of Scout Mash Podcast is brought to you by folks from Conahoe Creek Outfitters.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
They know camping and they know scouts. They aren't scouts

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And they know how to get you great gear and great prices for all your camping adventures.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:22,000
So go visit them at conahoecreakcom, at conohocreakcom.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
And now the old scout master.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
I'm okay, You know, card carrying number, scout nerd.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
We did that, We got. we're a scout nerd. Can they add scout nerd?

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Wait a minute, You have a card.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Ding ding, ding, ding, ding ding.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Hey, this is podcast number 173..

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast. This is Clark Green.

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Let's take a look in the mailbag here.

13
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Kim Jones is in California with Troop 723.

14
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
She wrote in to say thank you so much for keeping us all up to date on membership change issues.

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
I hope and pray everyone will keep their hearts open and do what's best for our youth.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
I hope we can let all youth participate in the best program in the world. again,

17
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Thanks for all you do.

18
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Well. thank you, Kim.

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Kim, that sentiment was echoed by any number of people who've contacted me over the past few weeks.

20
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
I sure do appreciate it and I join you in your aspiration.

21
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Let's keep our hearts and our minds open and do what's best for our youth.

22
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Malcolm Dickinson is a Roundtable Commissioner in Stamford, Connecticut,

23
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
and he wrote in to say my compliments on your thorough and thoughtful blog and website.

24
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
I really enjoy it and appreciate all you offer to Scouters.

25
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Malcolm went on to help me out and joined the Red Pencil Patrol.

26
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Now, if you're not aware of what the Red Pencil Patrol is,

27
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,000
I have a very sharp-eyed, astute group of proof readers out there

28
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
who notice problems in punctuation and spelling and things that I've gotten wrong,

29
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
and they're good enough to let me know about it so I can correct it.

30
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
I really do appreciate it.

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Proofing my own work has never been my strong suit.

32
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Malcolm went on to say: I've tried several times to make a donation

33
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
using the PayPal button on your support page but it didn't work.

34
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Well, Malcolm, I fixed that. If you go to support the blog at scoutmastercgcom,

35
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
you'll see how you can help support the blog in a financial way.

36
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
I really do appreciate that.

37
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Folks, in a moment we're going to hear a discussion about the immediate aftermath

38
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:03,000
of the membership policy change and it will likely be the last extensive discussion

39
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
we're going to have of it here on the podcast for a good long. while.

40
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,000
You know we're all in the business of delivering scouting to our scouts

41
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
and we have a lot of work to do. that isn't even remotely related to any of the issues

42
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
that have come out of this, but what I wanted to do here is discuss the way

43
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
that this policy change happened and some of the possible repercussions,

44
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
and offer some advice to fellow scouts who are dealing with issues surrounding those repercussions.

45
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Depending on a number of factors, you may have already moved on.

46
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
If you favored the change, it's likely that it has little to no effect

47
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
on what you're doing day to day in your work in scouting.

48
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
If you oppose the change, however, you may find yourself in a very confusing

49
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
or an off-putting set of circumstances and you aren't quite sure what to do

50
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
or how to resolve them, or you may have decided at this point

51
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
you can't continue offering your time as a volunteer.

52
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
I am here to do my level best to help my fellow scouts,

53
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Full stop, that's it,

54
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:11,000
And I do understand that this whole thing is causing a lot of difficulty for some of us,

55
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
So this is an open invitation: please,

56
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:21,000
If this change to membership policy has caused difficulty for you,

57
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
get in touch with me and I will do what I can do to help you out,

58
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
and you can email me at clark at scoutmastercgcom.

59
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
So the rest of the podcast is going to be a discussion I had

60
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
with someone who will be familiar to long-time listeners of the podcast, Frank Maynard.

61
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Frank is the author of Bob White Blather,

62
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
a blog chiefly concerned with Frank's role in scouting as a troop committee chair.

63
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
And we also had our old friend Walter Underwood,

64
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
who grew up in scouting and served in scouting for many years as a scoutmaster.

65
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
So I hope you'll find it all helpful and let's get started, shall we?

66
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
So we're going to have a little scouter discussion on this edition of the Scoutmaster podcast,

67
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And joining me is our friend Walter Underwood, out in Palo Alto, California.

68
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,000
How are you doing, Walter?

69
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
I'm doing fine.

70
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Yeah, Are the trees still as tall as they were the last time we spoke?

71
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Well, they are.

72
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
We actually have a couple of new trees. in the backyard

73
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
We have a western azalea, which is a native wild azalea.

74
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
It's a spray plant

75
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:30,000
And I'm sure the weather is disgustingly mild and wonderful.

76
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
I don't have to bring that up if you don't.

77
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:40,000
And also with me is Frank Maynard, and Frank is in Novi Michigan.

78
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
How are you doing Frank?

79
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Hello Clark, Good evening,

80
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
And our trees are just the right height here.

81
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Yes, I think that we're all very happy about the Michigan trees.

82
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
We had a season of flowering trees last year.

83
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
They all got bitten by the late frost and ended up with no apples and no cherries,

84
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
And this year we've had some just wonderful blossoms,

85
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And I'm hoping that the fruit comes back on the summer sometime.

86
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Walter, do we need to explain frost to you?

87
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
And I'm bored with that steady weather that you have.

88
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
You can get just about anything here.

89
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
We had frost last week, It's 82 degrees today,

90
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
And so it goes all over the map, especially in the spring.

91
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
That's right. folks

92
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Tune in to the Scout Master Podcast to hear old guys talk about the weather.

93
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
That's the best thing about it, I'll tell you.

94
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
I asked you to join me today so that we could discuss three things

95
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Concerning the recent change in membership policy that was announced by the BSA this past week.

96
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
that has been much in the news,

97
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:00,000
And I thought we'd start the discussion by just commenting about the process that happened

98
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Before we got started here. Walter, you were saying that it was a pretty unprecedented process for the BSA.

99
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Well then, it started this voice of the Scout process early on, about a year or so ago,

100
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
But this was definitely.

101
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:26,000
I got the impression that everything else- this became kind of number one, two and three priorities at National

102
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
And other stuff just didn't get done until it was finished

103
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,000
And probably for the rest of the year I think that's going to be the case.

104
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
What was your impression as you followed this, Frank?

105
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
What was happening in Novi and happening in Michigan?

106
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:49,000
Well, at least personally, I was interested to see the initial discussion that sort of came to everybody's awareness

107
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
back in February during the executive board meeting:

108
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Oh, they might change the policy and here's what they might change it to.

109
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
And then they voted and they decided to table it for three months and I thought,

110
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:09,000
well, it's probably for the best, because this is an awfully big change for this organization- to take on a simple vote of the executive committee.

111
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
It really needed input from a lot of people and I think they did a thorough job of doing that.

112
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
There wasn't a lot of talk about it in February when it initially started.

113
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:27,000
There have been discussions and meetings at the council level here where people could come and listen to.

114
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Basically, it wasn't a debate or it wasn't a public forum,

115
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
It was just a fact presentation.

116
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Here's what we're going to do and here's what we're going to propose, and or at least this is how we're going to go about finding out what to propose.

117
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
We had similar meetings in our council.

118
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,000
There was a series of four meetings in different areas of the council.

119
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:56,000
The public part of the meeting was more informative and then they did have a way for people to offer their opinion in writing about the whole thing.

120
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Was that happening out in your end of the country, Walter?

121
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:06,000
We did have the Fireside meeting and certainly got coverage in the local paper.

122
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:17,000
We had a reporter to that meeting and the reporter actually contacted people around trying to find someone who opposed the policy change and actually couldn't find anyone.

123
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
I'll probably tell you where we are at the whole spectrum.

124
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,000
We definitely did get in touch with people as much as possible.

125
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,000
They were certainly noticed. the survey went out.

126
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:50,000
I think probably the one group that might not have gotten really polled are those sort of 18 to 20 whatever-year-olds who are just less scouting than the youth and are off in college.

127
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
I know I tried to get my son to answer the survey but he was all busy doing stuff

128
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:04,000
In Walter's area in California. there the opinion was majority for the proposed change.

129
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
What was it like in Michigan?

130
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Well, in Michigan it was about mixed.

131
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
I think they leaned towards not changing it.

132
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:22,000
According to the report anyway, the councils in our area were more or less split, but I think that the analysis was that they leaned against the change

133
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:32,000
In our immediate area. here the balance was slightly in favor, although I'm taking a guess based on the people that I know

134
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:43,000
In our council, which is southeastern Pennsylvania. after analyzing the comments that people made, we were basically right around a 50-50 split.

135
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
We were as close to being 50-50 as you could be.

136
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
There's been a fair amount of discussion on both sides of the question.

137
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Our area was actually very nearly unanimous in favor of the change.

138
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
It's been a significant obstacle to recruiting in our area.

139
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
It's something I talk with basically every family that joins the group about.

140
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Just in discussions with district executives in our council and elsewhere in our region, we saw a measurable effect on recruiting, especially at the Cub Scout level,

141
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Because people told us the reason that they were not interested in the BSA was because of the existence of this policy.

142
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Were you aware of anything like that in Michigan, Frank?

143
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Not that I'm personally aware of

144
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:37,000
It really isn't much of an issue here and at least in the contacts that I've had, not at the unit level at any rate.

145
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:43,000
I don't think people are deterred by from joining scouting because of the policy.

146
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:52,000
I think the larger impact is when, of course, the Supreme Court decision came out back in I think it was 2000,,

147
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,000
and that kind of publicized the fact that there was a policy.

148
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:01,000
We had several instances where chartered organizations decided not to recharter

149
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
coincident with that, not necessarily giving that as the reason, but we kind of suspected.

150
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:12,000
We should mention here that we've got three reasonably different parts of the country represented.

151
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:17,000
We don't have anybody from the southern region of the BSA joining us today.

152
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,000
That's not because we consciously excluded anyone.

153
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
We just couldn't get anybody to be available for this discussion.

154
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:32,000
But let me do my best to represent what I know of that just through news reports and having read the materials released by the BSA-

155
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:43,000
The central and southern regions of the BSA. the general opinion was strongly opposed to changing the policy.

156
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
I wouldn't go so far as to try and state the reasons behind that.

157
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
I think they're reasonably self-evident:

158
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Michigan's central region,

159
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Of course, California western region and I'm in northeast region.

160
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:59,000
So hey, we got at least three regions represented.

161
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:05,000
That's not to say that we have any particular authority or inside scoop or knowledge.

162
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
We're basically just scouters like everybody else.

163
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:15,000
I know that. I've spent a great deal of time trying to understand what's going on and reading all the materials that have been released,

164
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
And I'm following the news after the decision.

165
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:26,000
This discussion is more or less just a casual discussion about what's going on in every unit across the country.

166
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Some notice is being taken of this decision.

167
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
In some places it's going to be much more difficult.

168
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Perhaps we're finding it.

169
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
Well. there's been a lot of people talk about it.

170
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
It's all about money or it's about this.

171
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
I don't think that was that at all.

172
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
If it was about money, this would have happened a long time ago.

173
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
National has really been listening.

174
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:53,000
I think that they were surprised at the impassioned response to the news from the executive council

175
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:59,000
And I think they responded really well and saying: okay, we're all going to talk,

176
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
We're going to work our way through this.

177
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
I think it may be affected by the region of the country that you live in,

178
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:15,000
But one of the perceptions that I've found kind of objectionable to me is this: policy change is simply the result of a lot of outside pressure,

179
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:19,000
The advancement of some kind of agenda or something like that.

180
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
What do you guys think about that?

181
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:28,000
In general, I don't think that the community had that much of a case one way or the other.

182
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
It just wasn't something that was a big deal around here. anyways,

183
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Walter, do you perceive this as the argument that I've read about again and again in some news reports and opinion pieces

184
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
is that the BSA caved into a lot of outside pressure?

185
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Well, I think they were talking about that pressure, but I don't think they caved in.

186
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:55,000
I think it was really people on the board. their companies went to non-discriminatory policies two or three years ago.

187
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:03,000
They say I can't say this at work and then say something else on this board.

188
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:07,000
My guess is it was a personal stance from people on the executive committee,

189
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
But that's a total guess.

190
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Obviously, when the ball got rolling, activists in the groups outside of the BSA did share their opinion,

191
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
but that was true of both sides of the question.

192
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:25,000
Last week, the BSA annual meeting convened.

193
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
How do you feel about the process that went on?

194
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:34,000
I think they did a good job of ensuring the security of the vote, at least from reading the voting procedures.

195
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:41,000
They wanted to make sure that each member of the board, each member who was entitled to vote,

196
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:48,000
got a chance to vote and got a chance to do so in confidence and make sure that their vote counted.

197
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:57,000
They had an outside organization do all the tabulating and it was done in a very somber manner. from what I can tell from having read about the proceeding,

198
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:06,000
I am not 100% clear on just who the delegates were and how many came from where and how one would get to become a delegate

199
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,000
if it was professionals, volunteers or how it was made up.

200
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
Maybe you can clarify that

201
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:17,000
Each council was allotted a number of voting members.

202
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:21,000
It was done by the number of members in the council.

203
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
We're a relatively small council.

204
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:29,000
We sent three voting members to the national meeting and they were all members of our executive board.

205
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:35,000
How the executive board decides on who those people are, I really haven't any idea,

206
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:39,000
But I know our council president and our council commissioner were two of those voting members.

207
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
I'm not too sure who the third one is.

208
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
It was a representative system.

209
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:47,000
I believe all 1400, whatever voters, were volunteers or whatever.

210
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
The voting members are all volunteer members.

211
00:16:50,000 --> 00:17:01,000
I don't believe that professionals get to vote in any situation in Scouts from your council all the way on up.

212
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:08,000
I want to say that I think they did a really good job of communicating what was going on in the annual meeting.

213
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:13,000
I have never heard much information about an annual meeting and I would like to hear more.

214
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
I think five years ago I probably didn't know there was an annual meeting

215
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:23,000
When we changed the uniform. did anybody hear anything about the annual meeting?

216
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:26,000
There's a lot of information out there, but I think you have to know where to look.

217
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:34,000
The resources that I use is Scoutwire, which is really sort of a blog or a news feed for professionals.

218
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
A lot of stuff gets distributed there,

219
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:44,000
A lot of changes or actions or proposals or new things that are available, revisions to publications and such

220
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:49,000
that you wouldn't necessarily get otherwise unless you paid attention to that feed.

221
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
I really admire the transparency of the process,

222
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:58,000
The fact that it was available to every single volunteer, not only in the meetings that were held locally,

223
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:07,000
but also in the surveys that went around. there was a very open invitation to send your opinion into the BSA.

224
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:12,000
I don't think I would have wanted to be the person on the other end of that email address.

225
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:19,000
I bet the pipes in Irving Texas, were clogged with bits for months.

226
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:23,000
If you'll look on the blog- and I'll make sure I link the podcast to it-

227
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:30,000
you'll find the information for voting members packet that was distributed at the national meeting.

228
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:34,000
How many pages is it, Frank?

229
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,000
It's 58 pages.

230
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:42,000
What it represents is all of the information gathered during the listening phase.

231
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
It's pretty thorough.

232
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:51,000
I really appreciated that kind of transparency and the ability to know those types of things.

233
00:18:51,000 --> 00:19:00,000
I'm glad that we weren't waiting in Vatican Square for the smoke to come out of the Sistine Chapel or anything.

234
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Absolutely. It was not secret.

235
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:08,000
Putting together a document that size is a lot of work. at National,

236
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:13,000
Somebody was busy for a couple of weeks putting that together.

237
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Well, if anybody down there is listening, thank you

238
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Absolutely.

239
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
That was a big job and a thankless job.

240
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:25,000
I'm sure that not a whole lot of people checked in to tell you what a wonderful job you were doing.

241
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:31,000
After seeing the product of it- not the nature of the discussion, not the nature of the decision,

242
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:34,000
but just the product of the information sharing-

243
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,000
I personally am really, really appreciative of that.

244
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:44,000
There have been literally hundreds of articles in news outlets and blogs and opinion pieces

245
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:48,000
and on television since the decision was made.

246
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:55,000
I think the tendency is to go towards the more sensational stories.

247
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:00,000
In one way, if I was just reading all of those articles,

248
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
I would think that there were a large number of organizations that are dropping the BSA like a hot potato.

249
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:13,000
Are you guys aware of any conflicts in your area as far as chartered organizations go?

250
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:22,000
I'm not aware of anything here that would result in a chartered organization dropping because of the membership policy.

251
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:32,000
I think that remains to be seen, because it's going to take some time for some of the other organizations to digest what this means.

252
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:38,000
Some churches may be more orthodox, so to speak, than others when it comes to the issue

253
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:42,000
and may feel that they no longer can support the organization.

254
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:48,000
I think what we need to be prepared for at the level is to have that happen

255
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:53,000
and to try to figure this out well before charter renewal time.

256
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:59,000
Certainly, our council organization, whether it be done by the professionals or the commissioners or whomever,

257
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:04,000
is going to have to try to get a handle on this early so that there are any changes

258
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:08,000
that need to be made in chartered organizations, that new ones can be identified

259
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,000
in plenty of time to ensure continuity for the units that are affected.

260
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
Walter, how about where you are?

261
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:18,000
You know of any conflicts or happenings with charter organizations. so far as this is concerned,

262
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
I don't know of any.

263
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:27,000
We have one charter organization that they think it didn't go far enough

264
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
because they were on the verge of dropping their troops

265
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:35,000
In our area. it kept the Boy Scout stock discriminating.

266
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:40,000
And the San Francisco Chronicle- that's going to be a pretty liberal voice.

267
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:48,000
Their editorial was good job- more work to do, but it was a very positive response to the decision.

268
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:52,000
I'm thinking that it's probably going to be a good thing for our area.

269
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:55,000
I haven't been made aware of any specific charter organization on our council

270
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:58,000
who's made a decision based on the policy change so far.

271
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:02,000
I won't be surprised to hear that there may be some in our council.

272
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:07,000
I have seen a couple of news stories about mostly evangelical churches

273
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:10,000
or those associated with the Southern Baptist Convention

274
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:15,000
who've already made a decision that they won't be continuing to sponsor scout troops.

275
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:20,000
There's actually a wonderful letter by someone from the Southern Baptist about

276
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:24,000
and it is that it's a matter of accepting the boys.

277
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:29,000
Membership policy may not be a real blocker for these organizations.

278
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:37,000
The Latter-day Saints have very publicly stated that they were supportive of the policy change.

279
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:40,000
It was drafted in a way that they could support it.

280
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:44,000
There have been a number of calls to continue to change membership standards

281
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:49,000
so that we include adults as well as youth.

282
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:53,000
There have been actually some pretty heated opinion pieces and editorials

283
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:57,000
that the Boy Scouts of America did not go far enough.

284
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:01,000
You're going to find a lot of people who do think that we didn't go far enough

285
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
and then there are others who are saying:

286
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
well, look what they did and what's next?

287
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:08,000
They're going to let adults in and they're going to let agnostics in

288
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
and they're going to let just about anybody in.

289
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:13,000
We're going to have Martians in the Boy Scouts before you know it.

290
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:17,000
Well, Walter will be happy to hear that because he is a Martian,

291
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:20,000
Absolutely. I have been called up at work

292
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:24,000
And I'm already in.

293
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:31,000
It's a huge issue and there's no way to handle this quietly

294
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
Big change. It's moving rapidly, faster than any of us would have expected.

295
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:39,000
It's a great decision with a couple of decades to go about this

296
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:43,000
and we have to decide. it's not about Gates, it's about the boys.

297
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:47,000
The question for any of us now, no matter what kind of conflict

298
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:51,000
we're in the midst of or difference of opinion,

299
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,000
is: uninterrupted service to our Scouts.

300
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
This is not something that they have any control over.

301
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:01,000
Whatever we can do to smooth the way and make it possible

302
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:04,000
for all boys to continue on in scouting,

303
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
that's going to be time well spent,

304
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:09,000
Definitely. When the decision was reached,

305
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:12,000
I sent an email out to our troop basically saying

306
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:16,000
the way we deliver the program to the boys does not change

307
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:21,000
Really. the impact at the boy is really almost insignificant.

308
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:24,000
Yeah well, one of the things that was shared with the voting members

309
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:27,000
was the result of the youth survey,

310
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:32,000
And the youth survey nationally was somewhere around

311
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:36,000
I think, two to one, that this is really not an issue

312
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
for our youth membership.

313
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
Frank, you've lit a little more close reading of that than I did.

314
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:45,000
The ages that they surveyed started at 14 or 16?

315
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:48,000
They started surveying at 16..

316
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:53,000
In fact, there was a lot of opposition to the BSA directly surveying youth members.

317
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:59,000
They did identify a group of youth that were willing to be interviewed

318
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
or surveyed for the purposes of it,

319
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:06,000
But they also used- I think it was the Harris organization-

320
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:12,000
a representative sample of youth in that age group, starting from 16 on up.

321
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:15,000
They did make the distinction between the youth who were members

322
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:19,000
and the youth who were not members as to their feelings on the issue.

323
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:22,000
I think overall I don't know that there was much difference between

324
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
scouts and non-scouts

325
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:28,000
And I think, as you said, about two-thirds didn't feel it was an issue

326
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:31,000
or were supportive of changing the policy.

327
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:35,000
Relatively few said we should not change the policy.

328
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:39,000
My kind of closing question: fellas, there are going to be

329
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:42,000
some interpersonal conflicts over this.

330
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:46,000
There are going to be people who disagree within units,

331
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
within councils and districts.

332
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,000
It's very likely that some people are going to discontinue

333
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:55,000
their volunteer relationship with the BSA.

334
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:59,000
What's your advice to any scouts who are out there

335
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:02,000
who are on the horns of a dilemma where they've got, maybe

336
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
in their unit or district or council.

337
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:07,000
they have some pretty hot arguments going on.

338
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:10,000
Be respectful and be polite and be understanding.

339
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:13,000
That's the best thing you can do.

340
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:17,000
If somebody undertakes to leave your unit,

341
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:21,000
you thank them for their service and you try to find a replacement.

342
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:26,000
There's really not much that you can do about the policy

343
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:29,000
having been changed. if you're a unit scouter,

344
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:32,000
if you're a committee chair or a scout master,

345
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:35,000
Basically, be aware of the facts and share the facts.

346
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
know what your council is doing about it.

347
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:40,000
know how your chartered organization feels about it

348
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:43,000
and whether or not they have any inkling

349
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:47,000
towards discontinuing the charter relationship.

350
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
But when it comes down to the personal level,

351
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:54,000
I think respect is the best route along those lines

352
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:58,000
and if somebody decides that they want to sever ties

353
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:01,000
with your unit over the issue,

354
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:06,000
thank them for their service and wish them well,

355
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:11,000
but do have the capability or at least think about replacing them

356
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
if they're in a key position.

357
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:16,000
Well, I've been doing this from the other direction

358
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:19,000
for a while, where we have people who are not comfortable

359
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,000
with the Scouts position.

360
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:26,000
I tell them that this is how I understand

361
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:29,000
the Scouts policy, the USA policy,

362
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:33,000
but I'm not telling you how to raise your children.

363
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:37,000
We are giving them great experiences in the outdoors.

364
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:40,000
We are encouraging the deeper understanding

365
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:44,000
of ethical choices.

366
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:47,000
We support your family and your choices.

367
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:49,000
That's what we do, That's what the Scouts do.

368
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:52,000
It's character, citizenship and fitness.

369
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:55,000
Respectful listening and respectful talk

370
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,000
is going to be the order of the day,

371
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,000
as it always has been.

372
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:02,000
We'll all weather whatever storm we have to weather.

373
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:05,000
I'm sure the BSA will be issuing some direction

374
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:08,000
and some information that deal with any issues

375
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
rising out of the membership change.

376
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:15,000
Until they have, I've got some resources.

377
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
You'll find them at ScoutmasterCGcom

378
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
and it's right there.

379
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
It's a menu item right at the top of the page now:

380
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
membership policy resources.

381
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
If you're listening and you know of any resources

382
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
that you think would be helpful for folks,

383
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:31,000
get in touch with me and I'll make sure to include them there.

384
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:33,000
Walter, thanks so much for joining us.

385
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,000
I'm glad to talk.

386
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:36,000
And, Frank, thank you,

387
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
My pleasure. Claire.

388
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:53,000
Well, thanks for listening

389
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:56,000
And once again, qualifying the discussion that we've had today.

390
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:59,000
you know this was a discussion between three Scouters

391
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,000
who are just like you.

392
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,000
We're just out there trying to do the best that we can

393
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
to deliver the program to our Scouts,

394
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:08,000
And if you're finding yourself in difficulties resulting

